## `Map` type_id is under-specified for proofs/testgen

Value::Map(BTreeMap<String, Value>) lost type parameter info when
MapStrStr was replaced with generic Map. Codegen can't serialize
Value::Map in general, and there's no way to express "map of string
to string" vs "map of string to json" at the port type level.

For proofs/testgen, need one of:
- Parametric type IDs: Map<String,String>, Map<String,Json>
- Type DAG / type expression structure instead of flat String type_id

Without this, "Map" is effectively "untyped JSON object."

Files:
  - core/ir/src/value.rs                (Value::Map definition)
  - core/ir/src/types.rs                (type_id is a flat String)
  - core/codegen/src/testgen/codegen.rs (can't serialize Map faithfully)

## `can_coerce_to` duplicates `satisfies` on Cardinality

can_coerce_to checks self.satisfies(target) then repeats the same
predicate. The intended distinction ("lossless injection") isn't
implemented. If coercions that alter cardinality but preserve
information are needed later (scalar→list by wrapping, optional→list
by empty/singleton), this needs explicit allowed-coercion rules,
not just set containment.

Files:
  - core/ir/src/types.rs  (Cardinality::can_coerce_to)

## Cardinality constants are flat — no compositional modeling

Named cardinality constants (ZERO, ONE, ZERO_OR_ONE, etc.) are syntactic
sugar for interval structs. If "everything is a DAG" then cardinality
constraints could be modeled as composable DAG nodes — making multiplicity
runtime-evaluable and composable with other constraint nodes rather than
a static port property. Currently cardinality is compile-time only (used
for test generation and port validation), not a first-class runtime concept.

Files:
  - core/ir/src/types.rs (Cardinality struct and associated constants)

